    title: "How Are Houston Gumbo Clay Foundations Repaired?",
    description: "Houston gumbo clay foundation repair starts with moisture stabilization, then underpins settled areas with piers to stable strata, relevels the slab, and corrects drainage.",
    h1: "How Are Houston Gumbo Clay Foundations Repaired?",
    answer: "Houston's gumbo clay is some of the most expansive soil in Texas, and it punishes foundations that were built too shallow, too thin, or without moisture control. When the distress shows up — cracked brick veneer, sloping floors, doors that will not close — the repair is not cosmetic. It is a structural intervention that puts the foundation back on stable support and fixes whatever let the soil move in the first place. Direct answer: the engineer first diagnoses the cause and stabilizes soil moisture, then underpins the settled areas with steel or concrete piers driven to stable bearing strata, lifts the foundation back toward level, and corrects the drainage and plumbing issues that caused the movement.\n\nDiagnosis comes before any pier is driven. The engineer surveys floor elevations to map the settlement pattern, reviews the original foundation type and the drainage around the building, and looks for the moisture smoking gun: a leaking supply line, a broken sewer, gutters dumping at the foundation, or a large tree desiccating one side. Gumbo clay moves with water, so a foundation that settled on one corner while the rest stayed put is usually telling a water story, not a soil-strength story. Repairing the structure without fixing the water guarantees a repeat performance.\n\nUnderpinning is the structural core of the repair. Steel push piers or helical piers are driven or screwed down through the active clay to a stable bearing layer, then brackets connect them to the existing grade beam or slab edge. Hydraulic jacks lift the settled sections back toward their original elevation — toward, not always to, because over-lifting cracks finishes and stresses the frame. The engineer specifies pier locations from the elevation survey, designs the bracket connections for the building's loads, and sets the lift sequence so the structure rises evenly. For interior settlement away from the perimeter, slab piers or mudjacking-style void filling may supplement the perimeter work.\n\nThe moisture fix closes the loop. Plumbing is pressure-tested and repaired, drainage is regraded to move water away from the building, and root barriers go in where trees threaten the foundation. In Houston's permitting landscape, foundation repair inside the city goes through Houston's permitting center with engineered plans, while unincorporated Harris County follows the county's residential repair permitting path with different submittal requirements. The authority having jurisdiction approves the work, and the engineer provides a post-repair elevation survey so the owner has a documented baseline for the warranty period.",
