    description: "Houston post-tensioned slabs are stiffened ribbed slabs with tensioned tendons engineered to bridge gumbo clay movement, detailed per the Post-Tensioning Institute method.",
    h1: "How Are Houston Post-Tensioned Slabs Designed?",
    answer: "The post-tensioned slab is Houston's signature foundation — the thin, steel-laced concrete plate under a huge share of the region's homes and light commercial buildings. It exists because of the gumbo clay: a conventional slab would crack as the soil swells and shrinks, but a post-tensioned slab is squeezed into compression by its tendons and stiffened by its ribs until it can bridge the soil's movement like a rigid raft. Direct answer: the engineer designs a stiffened slab with thickened edge and interior ribs, lays out high-strength steel tendons that are tensioned after the concrete cures, and sizes the stiffness and prestress for the site's predicted soil movement using the Post-Tensioning Institute method for expansive soils.\n\nThe design inputs come from the geotechnical report. The engineer needs the soil's swell potential, the depth of the active moisture-change zone, and the predicted differential movement — typically expressed as edge lift and center lift, the two ways an expansive-soil slab distorts. Edge lift happens when the perimeter soil swells while the center stays dry; center lift is the reverse. The PTI method turns these movements into required slab stiffness and prestress levels, and the engineer selects the rib depths, rib spacing, and tendon layout that deliver them. A Houston gumbo site with high swell potential gets a deeper-ribbed, more heavily prestressed slab than a mild site.\n\nThe tendons are the heart of the system. High-strength steel strands in plastic sheathing are laid in a grid through the slab before the pour, draped to follow the slab's bending profile, then tensioned with hydraulic jacks once the concrete reaches strength — usually within days. Tensioning squeezes the entire slab into compression, which does two things: it closes the shrinkage cracks concrete naturally wants to form, and it gives the slab the stiffness to span across localized soil swelling without bending to failure. The engineer specifies the tendon force, the elongation tolerances that verify it, and the sequence of stressing, because a tendon tensioned wrong is worse than no tendon at all.\n\nConstruction quality decides whether the design performs. Tendons must be placed at the designed depth and profile — a strand riding too high or too low changes the slab's behavior — and the concrete must reach the specified strength before stressing. The engineer requires inspection of tendon placement and reviews the stressing records with their measured elongations. In Houston's permitting world, residential post-tensioned slabs inside the city are reviewed by the city's permitting center against the adopted code and the geotechnical recommendations; in unincorporated Harris County, the county's residential permitting path applies with its own submittal requirements. The authority having jurisdiction approves the plans, and the design assumes the moisture detailing — drainage, barriers, and setbacks — will be built and maintained as drawn.",
