        heading: "Panels: From Casting Bed to Load-Bearing Wall",
        body: "Each panel is engineered for the stresses of its own construction. The lifting analysis treats the panel as a simply supported beam between crane pick points, and the engineer checks concrete stresses at the moment of initial lift  -  when suction between the panel and the casting bed adds to the panel's self-weight. Lifting inserts are positioned to keep those stresses within the concrete's early-age capacity, and the panel gets temporary strongback reinforcement where the analysis shows it is needed. The concrete mix and curing are specified so the panel reaches adequate strength before the crane arrives; lifting too early is a classic cause of panel cracking.\n\nIn service, the panel design checks vertical spanning under wind pressure, axial capacity under roof gravity loads, and the combined stress where they act together. Slenderness matters: tilt-wall panels are tall and thin, so the engineer checks buckling and second-order effects rather than treating the panel as a stocky column. Reinforcement is detailed for the full height, with extra steel around openings and at the base where the panel meets the foundation. Panel joints get engineered connections  -  welded plates, grouted dowels, or bolted embeds  -  sized for the shear and tension the wind analysis assigns to each joint.",
