        heading: "Buyouts, Elevations, and the Regulatory Layers",
        body: "The buyout program is flood mitigation by subtraction. The county identifies clusters of homes with repeated deep flooding, offers voluntary purchase, demolishes the structures, and deed-restricts the land as open space. Each buyout permanently removes future flood claims and emergency rescues from that property, and the aggregated parcels become linear green space along the bayous that doubles as conveyance and detention. Elevation takes the opposite approach for homes worth saving: the structure is raised onto piers or an extended foundation so the living space sits above the design flood elevation, with the lower area built to flood-resistant standards.\n\nRegulation prevents the next generation of flood losses. New development in Houston must meet detention and drainage standards demonstrating no adverse impact downstream, and structures in mapped floodplains must be elevated with flood-resistant construction below. The City of Houston administers these rules inside city limits through its development permitting, Harris County through its own regulations in unincorporated areas, and both coordinate with the Flood Control District's regional projects. Map updates periodically redraw the floodplain boundaries as better data arrives, which can change a property's requirements overnight. The authorities set and enforce the rules  -  and they alone decide permits and map determinations.",
